This is the documentation for the function tf.nn.sampled_softmax_loss:

"tf.nn.sampled_softmax_loss(weights, biases, labels, inputs, num_sampled, num_classes, num_true=1, sampled_values=None, remove_accidental_hits=True, seed=None, name='sampled_softmax_loss')
Computes and returns the sampled softmax training loss.

  This is a faster way to train a softmax classifier over a huge number of
  classes.

  This operation is for training only.  It is generally an underestimate of
  the full softmax loss.

  A common use case is to use this method for training, and calculate the full
  softmax loss for evaluation or inference as in the following example:

  ```python
  if mode == "train":
    loss = tf.nn.sampled_softmax_loss(
        weights=weights,
        biases=biases,
        labels=labels,
        inputs=inputs,
        ...)
  elif mode == "eval":
    logits = tf.matmul(inputs, tf.transpose(weights))
    logits = tf.nn.bias_add(logits, biases)
    labels_one_hot = tf.one_hot(labels, n_classes)
    loss = tf.nn.softmax_cross_entropy_with_logits(
        labels=labels_one_hot,
        logits=logits)
  ```

  See our [Candidate Sampling Algorithms Reference]
  (https://www.tensorflow.org/extras/candidate_sampling.pdf)

  Also see Section 3 of [Jean et al., 2014](http://arxiv.org/abs/1412.2007)
  ([pdf](http://arxiv.org/pdf/1412.2007.pdf)) for the math.

